  "3635a4b4d5eee1b9": "Adds text on a new line at the end of a memory file, without resending the rest; cheaper than memory_write for one new fact. Do not append a fact the file already has: change that line with memory_str_replace. Files are size-capped, so prefer editing and condensing over repeated appends. PRIVACY: never file, for anyone, even when asked: government-ID numbers (Aadhaar, PAN, passport, driving licence, voter ID, social security), payment-card or financial-account numbers (a card named only by its last four digits is fine); immigration status; caste; a minor user's own age, date of birth or school grade; sexual history or activity; sexual, physical or other abuse; criminal history, violence or crime-victim status; suicide, self-harm or disordered eating; conduct that breaks ArcNave's usage rules; health or personality inferences the user did not state. Stated health, sexual orientation, gender identity, race or ethnicity, religion, political views, union membership, disability and finances are filed only when the user asks you to remember that fact; otherwise leave them out. Omissions get no placeholder or reworded form.",
  "36e89ebdd3b2b331": "Changes one part of a memory file: old_str must match the file in exactly one place, spaces and line breaks included; widen it with surrounding text until it is unique. An empty new_str deletes it. Needs the version from your last memory_read or memory_write of the file. A failed match or a version conflict returns the current content so you can retry at once. PRIVACY: never file, for anyone, even when asked: government-ID numbers (Aadhaar, PAN, passport, driving licence, voter ID, social security), payment-card or financial-account numbers (a card named only by its last four digits is fine); immigration status; caste; a minor user's own age, date of birth or school grade; sexual history or activity; sexual, physical or other abuse; criminal history, violence or crime-victim status; suicide, self-harm or disordered eating; conduct that breaks ArcNave's usage rules; health or personality inferences the user did not state. Stated health, sexual orientation, gender identity, race or ethnicity, religion, political views, union membership, disability and finances are filed only when the user asks you to remember that fact; otherwise leave them out. Omissions get no placeholder or reworded form.",
  "5c4c7ea85bedffad": [
    {
      "description": "Shows tappable options to learn the person's preferences, constraints or goals before giving advice (a workout plan: goal, time, equipment; a book: genre, mood). Tapping is easier than typing on a phone.

First check the conversation: if the answer is already there or can be inferred, use it. If you are about to write clarifying questions as prose bullets, ask them with this tool instead.

Do not use it when the person asks "A or B?" (they want your recommendation), is venting, asks for your opinion, asks a factual question, needs written feedback, or already gave detailed constraints (proceed and state any assumption inline).

Always write a short message before the options. Prefer one question; three is a ceiling, not a target; each with 2 to 4 short, mutually exclusive options. Calling this ends your turn: the choice arrives as the person's next message, not as a tool result, so write nothing after it.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "properties": {
          "questions": {
            "description": "1 to 3 questions to ask.",
            "items": {
              "additionalProperties": false,
